2025-12-20 18:18:31,270 - __main__ - INFO - Content: ```javascript
function findMinSum(arr) {
    let dp = Array.from({ length: arr.length }, () => [...Array(arr.length).fill(0)]);
    
    function dfs(i, j) {
        if (i === arr.length || j === arr.length)
            return Math.min(dp[i][j]);
        
        const min_sum = Math.max(dfs(i + 1, j), dfs(i, j + 1));
        if (dp[i][j]) return min_sum;
        
        dp[i][j] = Math.abs(arr[i] - arr[j]) * 2;
        min_sum = Math.min(min_sum, dp[i][j] + dfs(i, j + -1) + dfs(i + -1, j));

        return min_sum;
    }

    return dfs(0, 0);
}
```

Explanation:
We use dynamic programming with memoization by creating two arrays called "dp" and "memo". We initialize both as empty arrays filled with zeros.

The main idea is to perform depth-first search from index i=0, j=0 until either i or j reaches the end of the input string. At any point during the DFS traversal, it checks whether there's already a calculated result stored in the DP table (`min_sum`). If not, we calculate the current minimal sum using the absolute difference between elements at indices i and j multiplied by 2, plus the sums obtained from performing DFS traversals on left side and right side respectively. Finally, after visiting all possibilities, we update our min_sum accordingly and continue recursively searching through remaining options. This way, we get the smallest total sum of Euclidean distances.
